# Start and Address Agents

## Overview

A live Agent runs under a Jido instance, and you need to find it again later:
by ID, by tenant partition, or through a stable reference that doesn't depend
on a process ID. This Livebook starts Agents under an application-owned
instance and addresses them each way.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:ok, ref} = Guides.Jido.agent_ref("acme-2")
{:ok, _pid} = Guides.Jido.start_agent_ref(ref, Guides.Counter)
{:ok, agent, []} = Guides.Jido.call(ref, signal)
```

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Define a Counter

```elixir
defmodule Guides.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "counter"

  agent do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/start-and-address")

    route "counter.increment", as: :increment, defaults: %{amount: 1} do
      action %{amount: amount},
        name: "increment",
        schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | count: context.agent_state.count + amount}}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

## Define an Instance

An application defines its own Jido instance with `use Jido`. The
`namespace:` is a stable, nonempty string that stable Refs and persistence
use. One namespace binds to one live instance per node.

```elixir
defmodule Guides.Jido do
  use Jido, otp_app: :guides, namespace: "guides/agents"
end

{:ok, _sup} = Guides.Jido.start_link()
Guides.Jido.namespace()
```

The result is `"guides/agents"`. In an application you add `Guides.Jido` to
your supervision tree instead of calling `start_link/0`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
children = [Guides.Jido]
```

`Jido.start_link/1` accepts these options:

| Option | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:name` | required | The instance name. `use Jido` sets it to the module. |
| `:namespace` | `nil` | Required for stable Refs and persistence. |
| `:max_tasks` | `1_000` | Task Supervisor child limit, or `:infinity`. |
| `:persistence` | `nil` | Default persistence adapter for this instance's Agents. |
| `:agent_server` | `[]` | Default Agent Server options, such as `turn_timeout:`. |
| `:debug` | `nil` | `false`, `true`, or `:verbose` semantic log detail. |

Unknown keys fail with `"Jido instance options contain unknown keys"`.
`use Jido` also reads `config :guides, Guides.Jido, ...`. Explicit options
override that config.

## Start by ID

`start_agent/2` takes a module or an instance plus options. Pass `id:`.

```elixir
{:ok, acme} = Guides.Jido.start_agent(Guides.Counter, id: "acme-1")
Guides.Jido.whereis_agent("acme-1") == acme
```

The result is `true`.

> #### Pass an ID {: .warning}
>
> `start_agent/2` without `id:` returns
> `{:error, %Jido.Agent.Error{code: :invalid_string, path: [:id]}}`.
> The function documentation says an omitted ID is generated. In beta.2 it
> isn't.

A live ID is unique within its instance and partition. A second start returns
the running process and changes nothing:

```elixir
Guides.Jido.start_agent(Guides.Counter, id: "acme-1")
```

The result is `{:error, {:already_started, pid}}` with the same PID as
`acme`.

Start options are the Agent Server options from
[Admission and Deadlines](agent-server-admission.md) plus `:id`, `:state`, and
`:partition`. An unknown key fails with `"unknown startup options"`.

## Start an Instance Value

You can also start an Agent value you already built. The Server keeps its ID
and state:

```elixir
{:ok, preset} = Guides.Counter.instantiate(id: "preset-1", state: %{count: 9})
{:ok, preset_pid} = Guides.Jido.start_agent(preset)

Jido.AgentServer.agent(preset_pid).state.count
```

The result is `9`. An instance value rejects `id:` and `state:` overrides
with `"Jido compatibility input is invalid"`.

## Partition by Tenant

A partition scopes IDs. The same ID can run once per partition:

```elixir
{:ok, tenant_b} =
  Guides.Jido.start_agent(Guides.Counter, id: "acme-1", partition: "tenant-b")

{
  tenant_b != acme,
  Guides.Jido.whereis_agent("acme-1") == acme,
  Guides.Jido.whereis_agent("acme-1", partition: "tenant-b") == tenant_b
}
```

The result is `{true, true, true}`. Lookups, listing, and counts default to
the unpartitioned scope:

```elixir
{Guides.Jido.list_agents(), Guides.Jido.agent_count(partition: "tenant-b")}
```

The result is a list with the `"acme-1"` and `"preset-1"` entries, and `1`.
`list_agents/1` returns `{id, pid}` pairs in no fixed order. `whereis_agent/2`
returns a PID or `nil`.

## Address With a Ref

A PID changes on every restart. A `Jido.Agent.Ref` is the stable identity
`{namespace, partition, id}`. It holds no PID, node, or module, and it isn't
a credential.

```elixir
{:ok, ref} = Guides.Jido.agent_ref("acme-2")
ref
```

The result is
`%Jido.Agent.Ref{id: "acme-2", namespace: "guides/agents", partition: nil}`.
Pass `partition:` to `agent_ref/2` for a partitioned Ref. An instance without
a namespace returns `{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{}}` with
`details.code: :jido_namespace_required`.

Resolve the Ref, start an Agent for it, and resolve it again:

```elixir
before = Guides.Jido.resolve_agent(ref)
{:ok, acme_2} = Guides.Jido.start_agent_ref(ref, Guides.Counter)

{before, Guides.Jido.resolve_agent(ref) == {:ok, acme_2}}
```

The result is `{{:error, :not_found}, true}`. `resolve_agent/1` returns:

| Result | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `{:ok, pid}` | A ready local Agent Server. |
| `{:error, :not_found}` | No live registration. |
| `{:error, :recovering}` | Registered, not ready yet. |
| `{:error, :unavailable}` | The Registry can't be read. |

Ref functions resolve the Ref on every call. They don't retry or replay:

```elixir
signal = Guides.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 3})
{:ok, agent, []} = Guides.Jido.call(ref, signal)

{agent.state.count, Guides.Jido.agent(ref).state.count}
```

The result is `{3, 3}`. The instance module also has Ref forms of `cast/2`,
`submit/3`, `status/2`, `cancel/2`, `attach_ref/3`, `touch_ref/1`,
`hibernate_ref/2`, and `stop_agent_ref/3`.

A Ref converts to a portable map for storage or messages:

```elixir
Jido.Agent.Ref.to_map(ref)
```

The result is
`{:ok, %{"id" => "acme-2", "namespace" => "guides/agents", "partition" => nil, "version" => 1}}`.

## Lower-Level Starts

`Jido.start_agent/2` calls `Jido.AgentServer.start/1`. Use the lower-level
forms when you need them:

| Function | Supervised by | Use it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Jido.start_agent/2` | The instance's DynamicSupervisor | The normal case. |
| `Jido.AgentServer.start/1` | Same | You already have options with `agent:` and `jido:`. |
| `Jido.AgentServer.child_spec/1` | Your supervisor | The Agent belongs in your own tree. |
| `Jido.AgentServer.start_link/1` | Linked to the caller | A bare worker with no Plugins, topology, or persistence. |

`child_spec/1` returns a `:transient` supervisor spec for the activation:

```elixir
{:ok, owned} = Guides.Counter.instantiate(id: "owned-1")
spec = Jido.AgentServer.child_spec(agent: owned, jido: Guides.Jido)
{:ok, app_sup} = Supervisor.start_link([spec], strategy: :one_for_one)

{spec.restart, is_pid(Guides.Jido.whereis_agent("owned-1"))}
```

The result is `{:transient, true}`. The Agent registers in the instance even
though your supervisor owns it.

`start/1` without `jido:` fails with `"start requires a Jido instance"`.
`start_link/1` without `jido:` or `runner:` fails with
`"expected a named Runic Runner in :runner"`, and it rejects runtime Plugins,
topology, and persistence with
`"runtime Plugins require a supervisor; use child_spec/1 or start/1"`.

## Stop the Example

```elixir
:ok = Supervisor.stop(app_sup)
:ok = Guides.Jido.stop_agent_ref(ref)
:ok = Guides.Jido.stop_agent("acme-1")
:ok = Guides.Jido.stop_agent("acme-1", partition: "tenant-b")
:ok = Guides.Jido.stop_agent("preset-1")
:ok = Jido.stop(Guides.Jido)
```

Each call returns `:ok`. `stop_agent/2` with an unknown ID returns
`{:error, :not_found}`.

